<manifest
    xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_MEDIA_IMAGES" />
    <!--
      READ_MEDIA_VISUAL_USER_SELECTED is deliberately NOT declared.

      Declaring it makes Android 14+ offer "Select photos…" (partial access)
      in the permission dialog. Partial access is structurally useless here:
      it exposes only the handful of images the user hand-picked, so the
      "Screenshots" album Shoto is built around simply isn't visible and the
      library comes back empty. Leaving it out gives the plain
      Allow / Don't allow choice, which is the only one this app can work
      with.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.USE_BIOMETRIC" />
    <!--
      Needed to drive the vibrator directly (see HapticsChannel.kt). Flutter's
      built-in haptics need no permission, but they route through
      HapticFeedbackConstants, which many Android skins map to nothing at all —
      so the app appeared to have no haptics whatsoever on a device whose
      system haptics were on and at full strength.

      This is a normal (non-dangerous) permission: it is granted at install
      time and never prompts the user.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.VIBRATE" />
    <!--
      Android 13+ only. Everything the app posts is one notification: the
      offer to keep a screenshot the user has just taken, at the moment they
      took it (see CaptureWatcher.kt). It is the least interruptive kind
      Android has — no sound, no banner, no badge — and the feature is off
      until switched on in Settings, so this is never requested by an app the
      user has not asked to be spoken to by.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />
    <!--
      Alarms are dropped when the phone restarts, so a reminder set for
      tomorrow does not survive a reboot tonight unless this app is told the
      phone came back. BootReceiver re-arms what is still in the future and
      forgets what is not — see ReminderScheduler.

      Note what is deliberately **absent**: SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM. Reminders use
      setAndAllowWhileIdle, which needs no permission and is allowed to slide a
      few minutes. The exact variant is a special-access permission Play
      reviews against a short list of genuine uses — alarm clocks, calendars —
      and "remind me about this screenshot this evening" is not one of them.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED" />
    <!--
      Predictive back: the gesture shows where it is going before it commits.

      Dragging from the edge shrinks the current screen and reveals what is
      behind it, and letting go early puts it back. Without this flag Android
      runs the legacy path instead — nothing moves under the finger and the
      screen simply swaps on release, which gives the one gesture with no undo
      no way to preview what it is about to do.

      **This is opt-in because it changes a contract, not a look.** The legacy
      path tells the app back happened *after* the fact; this one asks, before
      the animation starts, whether the app intends to handle it — so a screen
      that intercepts back has to answer up front. Both places that do are
      `PopScope`s that already answer synchronously from state
      (`screenshots_body.dart`, `quick_save_page.dart`), which is precisely
      what the new API needs; a screen that decided inside `onPopInvoked`
      would have had to be restructured first.
    -->
    <application
        android:label="Shoto"
        android:name="${applicationName}"
        android:enableOnBackInvokedCallback="true"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher">
        <!--
            **Portrait is declared here, not only in Dart**, and the splash is
            the whole reason.

            `SystemChrome.setPreferredOrientations` cannot run until the
            Flutter engine has started, and the window Android paints before
            that — LaunchTheme, drawn from the manifest — is under no
            instruction at all. Cold-starting the app while holding the phone
            sideways gave a landscape splash that snapped upright the moment
            Dart caught up. This attribute is the only thing that reaches that
            frame, because it is the only thing the system reads before any of
            this app's code exists.

            "portrait" and not "userPortrait": the latter lets a phone with
            auto-rotate on turn 180°, which is a rotation like any other.
            It matches `DeviceOrientation.portraitUp` in main.dart and
            `UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait` in Info.plist exactly — one answer
            in three places rather than three near-answers.

            `configChanges` keeps `orientation|screenSize` for the cases the
            system can still deliver one (a folding device changing posture,
            a desktop-mode window resize): the app handles it rather than
            being recreated.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTop"
            android:taskAffinity=""
            android:screenOrientation="portrait"
            android:theme="@style/LaunchTheme"
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|smallestScreenSize|locale|layoutDirection|fontScale|screenLayout|density|uiMode"
            android:hardwareAccelerated="true"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">

            <!--
                **No `io.flutter.embedding.android.NormalTheme` meta-data, and
                its absence is load-bearing.**

                That meta-data tells `FlutterActivity.onCreate` to swap
                LaunchTheme for NormalTheme itself. `installSplashScreen()`
                does the same swap, from `postSplashScreenTheme` in
                `values/styles.xml`, and it has to be the one that does it —
                the library reads the splash attributes off the theme that is
                current when it is installed, and holds the window until Dart
                says the handover is safe. Two mechanisms swapping the same
                theme at two different moments is a race with nothing to gain
                from winning it, so there is one.
            -->

            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER"/>
            </intent-filter>

        </activity>

        <!--
            The alternate launcher icons.

            Android has no "set my icon" API. What it has is one
            <activity-alias> per icon, each with its own android:icon and a
            LAUNCHER filter, of which exactly one is ever enabled — see
            AppIconChannel, which does the enabling and explains why it enables
            before it disables.

            Every one of these is android:enabled="false". MainActivity above
            is the default and is a real activity rather than a seventh alias,
            because a fresh install has to have a launcher entry before any of
            this app's code has run, and the manifest is the only thing that
            can promise that.

            targetActivity is MainActivity for all of them: an alias is not a
            second copy of the app, it is a second name and icon for the same
            activity, so whichever one is tapped the app starts once.

            android:exported must be true — a component with a LAUNCHER filter
            is by definition started from outside the app, and Android 12+
            refuses to install a package where one is not declared.

            The labels are all "Shoto". An icon is a colourway, not a different
            product, and a home screen where the name changes with the colour
            would be a different app every time.

            Generated assets: ic_launcher_<id>.xml is the adaptive descriptor
            and ic_launcher_<id>.png the API 24-25 fallback (minSdk is 24), all
            written by tool/generate_brand_assets.dart. Adding a variant means
            adding it to AppIcon.all, re-running that, and adding an alias here
            and a line in AppIconChannel.components.
        -->
        <activity-alias
            android:name=".IconTeal"
            android:targetActivity=".MainActivity"
            android:enabled="false"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="Shoto"
            android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_teal">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </activity-alias>

        <activity-alias
            android:name=".IconIndigo"
            android:targetActivity=".MainActivity"
            android:enabled="false"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="Shoto"
            android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_indigo">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </activity-alias>

        <activity-alias
            android:name=".IconPlum"
            android:targetActivity=".MainActivity"
            android:enabled="false"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="Shoto"
            android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_plum">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </activity-alias>

        <activity-alias
            android:name=".IconEmber"
            android:targetActivity=".MainActivity"
            android:enabled="false"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="Shoto"
            android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_ember">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </activity-alias>

        <activity-alias
            android:name=".IconMoss"
            android:targetActivity=".MainActivity"
            android:enabled="false"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="Shoto"
            android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_moss">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </activity-alias>

        <!--
            Sharing an image into Shoto lands here, not in MainActivity.

            Its own task (taskAffinity="") and excludeFromRecents are what
            make it behave like a sheet rather than a second copy of the app:
            it appears over whatever the user was doing, saves, and is gone
            without ever leaving a Shoto entry in the recents switcher.

            **This one deliberately carries no android:screenOrientation, and
            that is not an oversight — declaring it here would crash the app.**
            Android 8.0 (and only 8.0) throws
            `IllegalStateException: Only fullscreen opaque activities can
            request orientation` out of `Activity.onCreate` for any activity
            that is translucent — which ShareTheme makes this one — and carries
            a fixed orientation in the manifest. minSdk is 24, so 8.0 is a
            version this app still ships to, and the failure would be every
            share on it dying before a frame.

            The lock is applied in ShareActivity.onCreate instead, where it can
            be wrapped in the guard that version needs. See ShareActivity.kt.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".ShareActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTop"
            android:taskAffinity=""
            android:excludeFromRecents="true"
            android:theme="@style/ShareTheme"
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|smallestScreenSize|locale|layoutDirection|fontScale|screenLayout|density|uiMode"
            android:hardwareAccelerated="true"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">
            <meta-data
              android:name="io.flutter.embedding.android.NormalTheme"
              android:resource="@style/ShareTheme" />
            <!--
                SEND and SEND_MULTIPLE both, so picking several screenshots
                in the gallery and sharing them lands here as one sheet.
                Android sends the two as different actions, and an app that
                only declares SEND simply does not appear in the share sheet
                once more than one image is selected.
            -->
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.SEND" />
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.SEND_MULTIPLE" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <data android:mimeType="image/*" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!--
            **An activity this app ships but never wrote**, pinned to portrait
            like the two above.

            `url_launcher` contributes its own in-app browser activity to the
            merged manifest, and it arrives with no orientation of its own —
            an opaque, full-screen screen of this app that would turn sideways
            while every screen around it does not. The merged manifest is the
            only place it is visible; reading the app's own file would never
            have found it.

            Nothing reaches it today. Every `launchUrl` in the app either names
            `LaunchMode.externalApplication` or hands over a non-web scheme, and
            both leave for another app. But the fallback path exists —
            `platformDefault` on an http(s) URL asks for a Custom Tab and lands
            here when the phone has no browser that offers one — so this is one
            `mode:` argument away from being a live screen, and the day someone
            writes that argument is not the day to rediscover this.

            The plugin declares no `screenOrientation`, so the merger simply
            adopts this one; no `tools:replace` is needed and none is claimed.

            Safe to fix this way precisely because it is *opaque* — the
            Android 8.0 restriction that keeps ShareActivity out of the
            manifest does not apply.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name="io.flutter.plugins.urllauncher.WebViewActivity"
            android:screenOrientation="portrait"
            tools:node="merge" />

        <!--
            The Quick Settings tile. See ShotoTileService.kt for why the app
            wants one at all.

            `exported` and the BIND_QUICK_SETTINGS_TILE permission are both
            mandatory and they work together: exported lets System UI bind the
            service, and the permission means *only* something holding it can
            — so declaring it public does not make the tile invocable by other
            apps. Without the permission Android refuses to list the tile.

            No ACTIVE_TILE meta-data: that is for tiles that push their own
            state up (a VPN saying it connected). This one has no state to
            report, so the default listening model is the right one.
        -->
        <service
            android:name=".ShotoTileService"
            android:exported="true"
            android:icon="@drawable/ic_qs_shoto"
            android:label="@string/qs_tile_label"
            android:permission="android.permission.BIND_QUICK_SETTINGS_TILE">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.service.quicksettings.action.QS_TILE" />
            </intent-filter>
        </service>

        <!--
            Watches for newly captured screenshots. See CaptureWatcher.kt for
            why this is a job rather than a foreground service.

            `exported="false"` because nothing outside the app may start it,
            and the BIND_JOB_SERVICE permission is what lets the system's own
            JobScheduler do so — the same shape as the tile above, where
            exported plus a system-held permission means "the OS may reach
            this, nobody else may".
        -->
        <service
            android:name=".CaptureWatcher"
            android:exported="false"
            android:permission="android.permission.BIND_JOB_SERVICE" />

        <!--
            Fires one reminder. `exported="false"` because the only thing that
            may send it is the PendingIntent this app handed to AlarmManager,
            and a broadcast receiver another app can reach is a broadcast
            receiver another app can use to post notifications as Shoto.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".ReminderReceiver"
            android:exported="false" />

        <!--
            Re-arms reminders after a restart. This one must be exported —
            BOOT_COMPLETED comes from the system, not from us — which is
            exactly why the filter is narrow and the receiver does nothing but
            read its own stored list.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".BootReceiver"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.BOOT_COMPLETED" />
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>

        <!-- Asks Play services to fetch the OCR model at install time rather
             than on the first scan, which is what makes an unbundled model
             tolerable: without it the first search a user ever runs is the
             one that waits for a download. See docs/decisions/ml-models.md. -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="com.google.mlkit.vision.DEPENDENCIES"
            android:value="ocr" />

        <!--
          **Firebase does not get to start itself**, even though `main` now
          initializes it deliberately.

          The SDK ships a ContentProvider that Android runs during process
          creation, before any app code. Left in, it runs on *every* process
          — including the share sheet, which has no account and no navigation
          and must not pay for an SDK it never calls — and it fails there and
          says so in the log, because this app has no google-services.json on
          purpose.

          `main` passes explicit options from firebase_options.dart before the
          router is built, which is the only initialization that should ever
          happen. See lib/main.dart for why the ordering is a constraint
          rather than a preference.
        -->
        <provider
            android:name="com.google.firebase.provider.FirebaseInitProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.firebaseinitprovider"
            android:exported="false"
            tools:node="remove" />

        <meta-data
            android:name="flutterEmbedding"
            android:value="2" />
    </application>

    <!--
      Android 11+ hides other installed apps unless they are declared here.
      Without these, canLaunchUrl() reports false for perfectly working
      intents and the actionable-screenshot buttons all appear broken.
    -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.PROCESS_TEXT"/>
            <data android:mimeType="text/plain"/>
        </intent>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW"/>
            <data android:scheme="https"/>
        </intent>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.DIAL"/>
            <data android:scheme="tel"/>
        </intent>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.SENDTO"/>
            <data android:scheme="mailto"/>
        </intent>
    </queries>
</manifest>